The previous version only had a 1024-entry lookup table. Any message number at or above 1024, such as a manufacturer-specific one, fell back to a linear scan over `mesgs`. That scan usually ends in a miss, which is its worst case.

This version widens the table to all 65536 possible `FIT_UINT16` values (128 KB). Every call is now one bounds-free load plus a static-guard check, and the linear scan is never reached in normal operation.

Behaviour is unchanged:
- The table is filled in reverse order, so when several entries share a number, the first match still wins.
- Unknown numbers still return `NULL`.
- The fallback scan remains for the degenerate case `MESGS >= 65535`.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { LUT_SIZE = 65536 };
    struct Lut
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[LUT_SIZE];
        bool ok;
        Lut() : idx(), ok(MESGS < 65535)
        {
            if (!ok)
                return;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                unsigned n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < LUT_SIZE)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)(i + 1);
            }
        }
    };
    static const Lut lut;

    if (lut.ok)
    {
        unsigned i = lut.idx[num];
        return i ? &mesgs[i - 1] : NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```